From: Fabiano Tassotti <fabianotassotti@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: realtek: rework family D SDS13 PCS to actually pass traffic

Family D's CPU-facing SerDes (SDS13) needs two things beyond basic PCS
configuration to actually carry frames, not just to report link:

1. MAC6_SEL_SDS0, the bit that redirects the shared SDS_INDACS window
   to the family D SerDes instance, has to stay set through every
   subsequent write to SDS_MISC, including the final one that programs
   the PA enables and mode. A regmap_update_bits() mask that omits it
   lets the redirect get silently cleared on the way out.

2. The RTL8367S-VB receiver latches on a DISABLE -> HSGMII *edge* in
   SDS_MISC, not on the value, and only once the SoC-side UNIPHY has
   finished its own bring-up - empirically 3-25 seconds after boot,
   well after pcs_config()/pcs_link_up() run. Indexed addressing alone
   "lands" (PORT6_STATUS reads link=1/speed=2500M with the right index)
   but still carries no frames without this. A capped delayed-work
   loop, driven from mac_link_up(), redrives that edge and polls the
   SerDes status register until the receiver reports link.

This rework:

- Restores explicit indexed SDS_INDACS addressing (rtl8365mb_sds_write()/
  rtl8365mb_sds_read() take an index parameter throughout, family C
  passing 0 and family D passing SDS13's index), the approach validated
  on real hardware in commit b42847d2b4 (see
  ai-memory/mr80x-v5-family-d-8021q-wan-dhcp-20260813.md for the
  2026-08-13 WAN DHCP lease) - self-contained per transaction, rather
  than relying on a redirect bit set once and assumed to hold.
- Unifies rtl8365mb_pcs_config() into a single function for both chip
  families, sharing the tuning-table-apply loop, SerDes reset, BMCR
  reset and NWAY writes between family C and D.
- Includes MAC6_SEL_SDS0 in the final SDS_MISC write's mask and value.
- Adds the capped delayed-work re-latch loop described above.

Both fixes were found on different IPQ5018+RTL8367S-VB hardware
(TP-Link Archer AX55 v1) by Mieczyslaw Nalewaj (namiltd), krics and
gabonpivovich-web, discussed at
forum.openwrt.org/t/reduce-number-of-drivers-for-rtl8367s/237681
(posts #117-152), and landed as namiltd/openwrt branch Realtek_DSA2
(commit aae5c80e9d, co-developed with this author) plus GitHub PR
namiltd/openwrt#142.

Confirmed on real MR80X v5 hardware via TFTP RAM-boot: eth0's RX
counter and a round-trip `ping -I wan` both show genuine bidirectional
traffic, where the register sequence without these two fixes trained
the same link and logged the same output but carried nothing. Full
trail and sources in
ai-memory/mr80x-v5-family-d-sds13-traffic-fix-20260904.md.

